```
#include <string>

class Enemy
{
public:
    Enemy(std::string name, int health)
        : name(name), health(health), maxHealth(health) {}

    void takeDamage(int amount)
    {
        health -= amount;
        if (health < 0) health = 0;
    }

    bool isDead() const { return health == 0; }
    int getHealth() const { return health; }

private:
    std::string name;
    int health;
    int maxHealth;
};                      // <- this semicolon is easy to forget

Enemy goblin("Goblin", 30);
goblin.takeDamage(12);
goblin.getHealth();      // 18
```

- `public:` things anyone can use; `private:` only the class's own functions can touch. That's **encapsulation**: nobody can set health to -500 from outside.
- `const` after a function means "this doesn't change the object".
- `std::to_string(18)` turns a number into text, for building strings like `"Goblin (18 HP)"`.

In Unreal, a turret is a class too: `class ATurret : public AActor` with `UPROPERTY()` fields that show in the editor. It's this exact pattern with extra engine macros on top.

